<TopicPage slug="subjects/sports-science/advanced/biomechanics">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced lesson in Sports Science. It builds on the foundational lesson <em>Training
    Load and Adaptation</em> and on the intermediate lessons <em>Muscles and Energy Systems</em>,
    <em>The Heart and Lungs in Exercise</em> and <em>Strength and Hypertrophy</em> — you already
    know what the body's systems do and how they adapt. Here those systems are treated as a
    machine that physics can measure: levers, forces, momentum and drag.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain the body's third-class lever bargain — speed bought at the price of muscle force — and compute a joint's force bill;</li>
    <li>apply the impulse-momentum theorem to landings and strikes, and say why "land soft" is arithmetic, not style;</li>
    <li>describe how ventilation, cardiac output and VO<sub>2</sub>max set the ceiling on aerobic effort;</li>
    <li>read the force-velocity curve, and explain why training shifts the curve rather than moving you along it;</li>
    <li>use conservation of angular momentum to explain tucks, skater spins and take-off placement.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Biomechanics is the engineering ladder applied to the moving body: bones as levers, muscles
    as engines, the heart and lungs as the fuel-delivery system, and every sport skill as a
    physics problem the athlete solves with timing. It is the analytical half of the anatomy and
    physiology unit — the skeletal, muscular, respiratory and circulatory systems are first the
    machinery of movement, and biomechanics is how that machinery is measured, modelled and
    coached. The payoff is that technique stops being folklore: "get low on landing" is not a
    preference, it is the impulse-momentum theorem with a person attached.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="levers, force–velocity, technique physics, equipment" widget="equationStep" config={biomechCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four panels — each one attaches a physics
    equation to a coaching cue. Watch the same theme recur: every panel is a trade-off (speed
    against force, force against velocity, energy return against regulation), and each trade is
    settled by measurable mechanics rather than preference.
  </p>

  <h2>The body as a lever system: skeleton and muscle</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>skeletal system</strong> supplies rigid segments (levers) and pivot points
    (joints); the <strong>muscular system</strong> supplies the forces, pulling on bones via
    tendons that insert a few centimetres from the joint axis. Most limb joints act as
    <strong>third-class levers</strong> — the muscle's force sits between the fulcrum and the
    load — which trades mechanical advantage for speed and range. Worked example, holding a
    2 kg ball in the hand: the biceps inserts about 5 cm from the elbow; the ball sits about
    35 cm out. Balancing torques about the elbow: F<sub>biceps</sub> × 0.05 m = (2 × 9.81) ×
    0.35 m ≈ 6.9 N·m, so the biceps must pull with about 137 N — roughly seven times the ball's
    weight (and more, once the forearm's own ~15–20 N at its centre of mass is included). The
    same geometry gives the reward: the hand moves through a 7-times larger arc at 7 times the
    muscle's shortening speed. This is the central bargain of human anatomy — we chose velocity
    over force, and the force bill is paid by muscle cross-section. It also explains injury
    patterns: the tissues at the joint (tendons, insertions) see forces many times the external
    load, which is why eccentric strengthening and gradual load progression protect them.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the skeleton and muscles form a lever system that deliberately
    sacrifices force for speed, and the bill arrives as large joint forces. Next, the laws
    that govern every skill performed with the whole body — and the single most useful
    equation a coach can carry.
  </p>

  <h2>Newton's laws on the field</h2>
  <p>
    Every sport skill is Newton with a stopwatch. <strong>First law (inertia):</strong> a 100 kg
    scrum is hard to stop because it is hard to stop. <strong>Second law (F = ma):</strong>
    acceleration comes from net force — a sprinter's first strides are almost entirely horizontal
    force production against the blocks and ground. <strong>Third law (action–reaction):</strong>
    the only way to accelerate is to push the planet — every jump is the ground pushing back
    against the force you apply, and the measure of that push, the <strong>ground reaction
    force</strong> (GRF), is what force plates record. Elite sprinters produce peak GRFs of
    roughly 3–5 times body weight at footstrike, and their skill is partly the ability to apply
    large horizontal force <em>briefly</em>: at top speed, ground contact lasts under about
    0.09 s.
  </p>
  <p>
    The most useful single equation in sports biomechanics is the impulse-momentum theorem,
    F·Δt = Δ(mv): the change in momentum equals force multiplied by the time it acts. Worked
    example, landing from a jump: a 70 kg athlete lands with a downward velocity of about
    3.1 m/s (dropped from 0.5 m). Stiff-legged, stopping in 0.05 s, the average GRF is
    70 × 3.1 / 0.05 ≈ 4,400 N — over six times body weight, all through knees and spine.
    Bending the hips, knees and ankles extends the stopping time to 0.2 s: the same momentum
    change now costs about 1,100 N, under twice body weight. "Land soft" is this arithmetic,
    and it is also the logic of crash mats, striking with a "give" in the wrist, and catching a
    ball by drawing the hands back.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "stiff landings are fine if you are strong enough." It seems
  reasonable because strength training raises force capacity, so absorbing the impact quickly
  looks like a feat of strength. But the impulse-momentum theorem fixes the total payment:
  the same momentum change costs about 4,400 N stopped in 0.05 s and about 1,100 N stopped in
  0.2 s — strength does not change the bill, only the speed of payment changes its size. The
  correct picture: bending the hips, knees and ankles is not weakness, it is force management,
  and it is why crash mats and drawing the hands back when catching work.</p>

  <h2>The delivery systems: respiration and circulation under load</h2>
  <p>
    Muscles are combustion engines, and two systems feed them. The <strong>respiratory
    system</strong> raises its delivery dramatically with demand: breathing goes from about 12
    breaths per minute at rest (tidal volume ~0.5 L, total ventilation ~6 L/min) to 40–50
    breaths per minute with tidal volumes of 2–3 L during maximal exercise — ventilation above
    100 L/min. Oxygen crosses into the blood at the alveoli, and the <strong>circulatory
    system</strong> delivers it: cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume, rising from about
    5 L/min at rest to 20–30 L/min in most people and above 35 L/min in elite endurance
    athletes (whose stroke volumes can exceed 160–200 mL per beat). The ceiling of the whole
    chain is VO<sub>2</sub>max — the maximal oxygen uptake, typically 35–45 mL/kg/min untrained,
    70–85+ in elite male distance runners — and it is the best single physiological index of
    aerobic fitness, because every gram of ATP made aerobically is billed in oxygen. These
    systems are why warm-ups exist: they raise heart rate, dilate vessels and open lung
    capacity <em>before</em> the effort, so the first sprint is not paid for out of an
    unprepared system.
  </p>

  <h2>Energy supply and the effects of exercise on the body</h2>
  <p>
    The fuel side completes the picture. The <strong>ATP-PC system</strong> powers about the
    first 10 seconds of all-out effort; <strong>anaerobic glycolysis</strong> covers roughly 10
    seconds to 2 minutes and accumulates metabolites associated with fatigue; the
    <strong>aerobic system</strong> carries everything longer, using carbohydrate and fat with
    the oxygen the lungs and heart delivered. Exercise then remodels the machinery: consistent
    aerobic training increases stroke volume and capillary density and raises mitochondrial
    content (endurance adaptations); resistance training adds contractile protein and tendon
    stiffness (hypertrophy, visible as increased muscle cross-sectional area); and both raise
    baseline health markers — resting heart rate falls, blood pressure improves, insulin
    sensitivity rises. The immediate effects are equally mechanical: post-exercise oxygen
    consumption (EPOC) is the respiratory system repaying the oxygen debt; delayed-onset muscle
    soreness follows unaccustomed eccentric work, which is microdamage being repaired —
    supercompensation felt from the inside.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the delivery systems set the aerobic ceiling while the fuel systems
    and training adaptations set how fast the machinery can work. Next, the trade-off that
    shapes every sport skill — and why technique is physics you can film.
  </p>

  <h2>The force–velocity trade and technique as physics</h2>
  <p>
    Muscle obeys a trade-off: the force it can produce falls as its shortening speed rises —
    maximal force at zero speed (isometric), maximal speed under minimal load. Sports sit at
    points on this curve (a powerlifter at the slow-heavy end, a sprinter at the fast-light
    end), and training shifts the whole curve upward rather than moving the athlete along it —
    which is why maximal-strength work helps sprinters and velocity-specific work helps
    lifters, each buying capability at the other end. Technique analysis applies the same
    mechanics to whole-body skills. The <strong>kinetic chain</strong>: powerful throws
    sequence ground → legs → hips → trunk → shoulder → arm like a whip, each segment
    decelerating as it transfers momentum to the next — the arm does not "muscle" the ball, it
    is the last link in a chain that started at the floor. <strong>Projectile motion:</strong>
    without air resistance the optimal release angle for range is 45°, but real implements have
    aerodynamics — a javelin is thrown nearer 33–35° because lift extends its flight, and a
    shot (dense, slow in air) is best near 41–42° because release height shifts the optimum
    below 45°. <strong>Angular motion:</strong> a diver leaves the board with fixed angular
    momentum; tucking cuts the moment of inertia and angular velocity rises to conserve L = Iω
    — the tuck is not "spinning harder", it is shrinking the radius so the same angular
    momentum spins faster.
  </p>

  <h2>Angular momentum and rotational sport</h2>
  <p>
    Rotational skills deserve their own accounting, because their conservation law produces
    coaching's most counter-intuitive cues. Angular momentum L = Iω is conserved in flight:
    moment of inertia I (how mass is distributed about the axis) times angular velocity ω (how
    fast it spins). A gymnast leaving the vault has whatever L the take-off gave them — no more,
    no less, until landing. Tucking pulls mass toward the axis: I falls by more than half, so ω
    more than doubles, and a slow pitch becomes a fast somersault; extending out of the tuck
    reverses the trick, converting spin back into controllable slowness for the landing. The
    same law explains a figure skater's spin (arms in: I down, ω up — measured spins exceed 300
    degrees per second), a diver's pike, and why a falling cat rights itself by rotating its
    two halves in opposite senses while net L stays zero. On the ground, rotation is
    <em>created</em>, not conserved: ground reaction force applied off-centre through the body's
    centre of mass generates the torque that starts every dive, high-bar swing and judo throw —
    which is why take-off foot placement, not arm effort, decides the difficulty a gymnast can
    attempt.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "tucking makes you spin by adding spin — you try harder in
  the air." It feels reasonable because the tuck is the visible effort and the spin visibly
  accelerates the moment you pull in. But angular momentum L = Iω is conserved in flight:
  nothing the gymnast does airborne changes L. The correct picture: tucking shrinks the moment
  of inertia, so angular velocity must rise to keep the product constant — a slow pitch becomes
  a fast somersault with zero new effort. That is why take-off foot placement, not air-side
  striving, decides how much rotation is available, just as a skater's arms in, rather than a
  harder push, accelerate an established spin.</p>

  <h2>Equipment rides the same equations</h2>
  <p>
    Drag force scales with the square of speed, F = ½·ρ·C<sub>d</sub>·A·v², and with it the
    power needed to overcome drag scales with the cube, P ∝ v³. Worked example, cycling: at
    10 m/s with a realistic C<sub>d</sub>A of 0.3 m², drag is ½ × 1.2 × 0.3 × 100 ≈ 18 N, so
    the power to beat the air is 18 × 10 = 180 W. Double the speed to 20 m/s and drag quadruples
    to 72 N — power = 1,440 W, eight times the power for twice the speed. This is why aero
    positions, skinsuits and drafting dominate cycling time-trialling, and why marginal gains
    live in aerodynamics rather than in weight (on flat ground at speed, air resistance
    overwhelms gravity's toll). The same equation prices swimming (drag reduction through body
    position is worth more than raw power) and explains the "super shoe" era: modern marathon
    foams return more of each landing's energy (measured marathon gains of roughly 2–4%),
    prompting World Athletics to cap stack heights and enforce sole-thickness rules — regulation
    chasing materials science in real time. Racket design hides an elegant detail: the "sweet
    spot" is literally a vibration node, the point where impact excites the least frame
    vibration and transfers the most energy to the ball.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "stronger muscles automatically mean better performance."
    Force is only one term in the performance equation; speed, timing and technique determine
    <em>when and where</em> the force arrives. The kinetic chain example makes it concrete:
    throwing velocity correlates poorly with arm strength alone and well with sequential timing
    — the fastest throwers are those who delay the arm best, not those with the biggest
    shoulders.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why do most limb joints act as third-class levers, and what does that arrangement cost and buy?</li>
    <li>A 70 kg athlete lands at 3.1 m/s. One stops in 0.05 s, one in 0.2 s. Compute the average ground reaction force in each case.</li>
    <li>Why can a gymnast who has left the vault not spin faster by "trying harder" in the air — and what actually determines the available rotation?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The muscle inserts a few centimetres from the joint, between
    fulcrum and load: the cost is mechanical advantage (the biceps pulls about seven times the
    ball's weight to hold 2 kg), the reward is speed and range — the hand sweeps a seven-times
    larger arc. (2) F·Δt = Δ(mv): 70 × 3.1 = 217 N·s of momentum, so 217 ÷ 0.05 ≈ 4,400 N
    (over six times body weight) versus 217 ÷ 0.2 ≈ 1,100 N (under twice body weight) — the
    momentum change is fixed, only the payment schedule differs. (3) Angular momentum is
    conserved in flight, so airborne effort cannot add rotation; only tucking (shrinking I)
    converts existing L into faster spin, and the take-off's off-centre ground reaction force
    is what created L in the first place.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Bones are levers and joints are fulcrums; third-class levers trade force for speed and range.</li>
    <li>Impulse-momentum, F·Δt = Δ(mv), turns "land soft" into arithmetic: longer stopping time, smaller force.</li>
    <li>Ground reaction force is the athlete's only way to accelerate — every jump is the ground pushing back.</li>
    <li>Ventilation, cardiac output and VO<sub>2</sub>max set the aerobic ceiling; energy systems set the timescale.</li>
    <li>The force-velocity curve is a trade-off; training shifts the whole curve rather than sliding along it.</li>
    <li>Angular momentum is conserved in flight — tucks shrink I to raise ω, and take-off placement creates L.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The natural next step in this subject is <em>Periodisation and Programming</em>, which turns
    these mechanical and physiological limits into training plans that respect them. The physics
    used here — statics, impulse, drag — is developed fully in the physics ladder's lessons on
    motion, momentum and fluids.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Measurement: from coach's eye to 3-D model.</strong> Modern biomechanics quantifies
    what the eye guesses: marker-based motion capture reconstructs 3-D joint angles and segment
    velocities at 100–500 frames per second; force plates measure the GRF vector underfoot;
    combining them yields joint moments and power via inverse dynamics — the full mechanical
    story of a jump or throw. Wearables democratised a subset: GPS vests log distance,
    sprint counts and accelerations; single-camera video plus pose-estimation models now
    approximate lab kinematics on a phone. <strong>Injury mechanics:</strong> non-contact ACL
    ruptures have an identified signature — deceleration or cutting with knee valgus (knee
    collapsing inward) under high GRF; neuromuscular prevention programmes built on that
    mechanism (FIFA 11+ and descendants) cut ACL and other injury rates by roughly 30–50%
    where properly adopted — biomechanics as measured public health. <strong>Open
    questions:</strong> the individuality of technique (is there an optimal sprint pattern, or
    a family of optima scaled to each athlete's force-velocity profile?); footwear's long-term
    effects on tissue loading; and whether markerless capture can reach the accuracy threshold
    where clinical gait analysis runs from a phone video. The physics is settled; the
    athlete-specific optimisation is the live research frontier.
  </p>

